  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MULTI-HABITAT CONSERVATION OF NMBS IN EC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate change and habitat loss in the Neotropics are critical threats to migratory birds. Climate change may promote upslope movements of many bird species, including migratory birds. Deforestation is higher in Ecuador than elsewhere in South America. The increasing frequency and severity of wildfires pose a growing threat to biodiversity. To effectively protect neotropical migrants long term, this project will address the current habitat loss and climate change crisis across seven protected reserves covering a total area of 30,265 acres along a broad altitudinal gradient. First, partners will reduce current threats by patrolling seven reserves. Second, they will monitor migratory birds within the targeted reserves to understand their distribution and abundance to inform reserve expansion. Third, they will add 685 acres of higher elevation forest to Buenaventura Reserve and 246 acres to Jorupe Reserve in southern Ecuador. Fourth, they will plant 22,320 native trees in Tropical Rainforest ecosystem to enrich habitat quality and connectivity among remaining habitat patches. Lastly, they will increase protection against forest fires at five vulnerable reserves by building the capacities of staff and local communities. The proposed activities will benefit over 20 migratory birds, including Buff-breasted Sandpiper, Olive-sided Flycatcher, and Canada Warbler.</narrative>
  </description>
